---
import ToolLayout from '../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import AdSlot from '../components/AdSlot.astro';
import SplitPdf from '../islands/SplitPdf';

const faqs = [
  { q: 'How do I write page ranges?', a: 'Separate ranges with commas: <code>1-3, 5, 8-10</code>. An open range like <code>8-</code> means “page 8 to the end”. Each range becomes its own PDF unless you tick “Put all ranges into one PDF”.' },
  { q: 'Can I extract just a few pages?', a: 'Yes. Choose <strong>Select pages</strong>, tap the pages you want, and you’ll get one PDF containing only those pages, in their original order.' },
  { q: 'How do I save every page as a separate PDF?', a: 'Choose <strong>Every page</strong>. PDFSherpa creates one PDF per page and bundles them into a single .zip download.' },
  { q: 'Does splitting reduce quality?', a: 'No. Pages are copied as they are, so text, images and links are unchanged.' },
  { q: 'Is my PDF uploaded?', a: 'No. Splitting runs entirely inside your browser, so the document never leaves your device.' },
  { q: 'Can I split a password-protected PDF?', a: 'Not directly. Open it with its password, save an unprotected copy, then split that copy.' },
];
---
<ToolLayout
  slug="split-pdf"
  title="Split PDF Online Free – Extract Pages from PDF | PDFSherpa"
  description="Split a PDF by page ranges, extract selected pages, or save every page as its own PDF. Fast, free and private: files never leave your device."
  h1="Split PDF"
  lead="Split a PDF by page ranges, pull out just the pages you need, or save every page as its own file. Nothing is uploaded."
  howToTitle="How to Split a PDF"
  steps={['Select or drop your PDF.', 'Choose <strong>By ranges</strong>, <strong>Select pages</strong> or <strong>Every page</strong>.', 'Click <strong>Split</strong> and download your PDF or .zip.']}
  faqs={faqs}
  related={['merge-pdf', 'organize-pdf', 'rotate-pdf', 'compress-pdf']}
>
  <SplitPdf client:load />

  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>Three ways to split</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>By ranges:</strong> type something like <code>1-3, 5, 8-</code>. You get one PDF per range: pages 1–3, page 5, and page 8 to the end. Tick the box to combine the ranges into one file instead.</li>
      <li><strong>Select pages:</strong> tap thumbnails to pick exactly the pages you want. Use <em>All</em> or <em>None</em> to speed things up.</li>
      <li><strong>Every page:</strong> each page becomes its own PDF, named <code>yourfile-page-1.pdf</code>, <code>yourfile-page-2.pdf</code> and so on, in one .zip.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Step by step</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Open your PDF. Thumbnails of every page appear so you can see what you’re picking.</li>
      <li>Choose a split mode and enter your ranges or tap your pages.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Split</strong>. One output downloads as a PDF. Several outputs come as a .zip, and you can also save files one by one from the list.</li>
    </ol>

    <AdSlot variant="inline" slot="tool-article" />

    <h2>Examples</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Send only the signed page of a contract:</strong> Select pages → tap the signature page → Extract.</li>
      <li><strong>Break a 300-page textbook into chapters:</strong> By ranges → <code>1-24, 25-61, 62-110, 111-</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Upload each certificate separately:</strong> Every page → download the zip.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Want to remove pages or change their order instead? Use <a href="/organize-pdf">Organize PDF</a>. Need a smaller file after extracting?
      Try <a href="/compress-pdf">Compress PDF</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Limitations</h2>
    <p>
      Encrypted PDFs must be unlocked by their owner first. Bookmarks that point to pages you leave out are dropped. Thumbnails for very long documents
      appear progressively, but you can split straight away without waiting for them.
    </p>

    <h2>Privacy</h2>
    <p>
      Splitting happens in your browser with the open-source pdf-lib library. Your PDF is never uploaded, logged or stored, which is safe for contracts,
      medical records and anything else you wouldn’t email to a stranger.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
